**TL;DR:** Signals are synchronous, glitch-free values for UI state; RxJS remains for async streams — interop via toSignal/toObservable.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Signals = sync UI state; RxJS = async event streams</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Signals</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">signal/computed/effect</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">sync, glitch-free</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">zoneless CD</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">RxJS</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">Observable, operators</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">async streams, cancel</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">interceptors</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Interop</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">toSignal(obs$)</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">toObservable(sig)</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">choose per need</text></svg></div>

Signals (`signal(0)`, `computed(()=>count()*2)`, `effect(()=>log(count()))`) are synchronous, pull-based values. Writing a signal synchronously updates any `computed` that reads it without extra ticks. Angular's new zoneless change detection subscribes at read points, so only consumers re-render — no zone.js dirty check.

RxJS (`Observable`, `Subject`, `switchMap`, `debounceTime`) excels at modeling async event streams, cancellation, and time-based operators (HTTP, websockets, drag). Replacing all RxJS with signals loses operator richness.

Guidance: use Signals for component local/UI state and derived values; use RxJS for HTTP, forms `valueChanges`, and inter-component event buses. Bridge with `toSignal(this.http.get(...), {initialValue:null})` and `toObservable(mySignal)`.

| Criterion | Signals | RxJS |
|---|---|---|
| Nature | sync value (`sig()`, `sig.set`) | async stream (`subscribe`, `pipe`) |
| Derived | `computed(()=>...)` cached | `map`, `combineLatest` |
| Side effect | `effect(()=>...)` (no signal writes) | `tap`, `subscribe` |
| Interop | `toSignal` / `toObservable` | same |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I use signals for UI counters/flags and computed, RxJS for HTTP/websockets — and bridge via toSignal for HttpClient.'

```ts
import { signal, computed, effect } from '@angular/core';
import { toSignal } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({ template: '{{ count() }} {{ doubled() }}' })
export class CounterComponent {
  count = signal(0);
  doubled = computed(() => this.count() * 2);
  constructor(){ effect(()=> console.log(this.count())); }
  // RxJS remains for HTTP
  users = toSignal(inject(HttpClient).get('/api/users'), { initialValue: [] });
  inc(){ this.count.update(v=>v+1); } // no .next(), synchronous
}
```

**TL;DR:** bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {providers:[...provideRouter, provideHttpClient]}) replaces platformBrowserDynamic + AppModule.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Standalone bootstrap: component + providers, no NgModule</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#ef4444"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#ef4444">Before: NgModule</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">AppModule + declarations</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">platformBrowserDynamic</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">imports array</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Standalone</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@Component standalone:true</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">imports: [OtherComp]</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">tree-shakable</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">bootstrapApplication</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">providers: [provideRouter]</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">provideHttpClient()</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">environmentProviders</text></svg></div>

Since Angular 17, standalone is default: `ng generate component --standalone` (now implicit). Components declare `standalone:true` and list dependencies in `imports:[CommonModule, ChildComponent]`. No `NgModule` declaration needed.

Bootstrap becomes:

```ts
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers:[ provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding()), provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor])), provideAnimations()]
})
```

Each `provide*` function is a thin wrapper over `EnvironmentProviders`. Lazy loading uses `loadComponent:()=>import('./admin.component')` or `loadChildren`. Tests use `TestBed.configureTestingModule({imports:[MyComponent]})` without declaring modules.

| Concept | NgModule era | Standalone |
|---|---|---|
| Component def | declared in NgModule | `standalone:true` + `imports` |
| Bootstrap | `platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule(AppModule)` | `bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {providers})` |
| Routing | `RouterModule.forRoot` | `provideRouter` |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I never create an AppModule in new projects — I compose providers via provideRouter/provideHttpClient in main.ts and lazy-load with loadComponent.'

```ts
// main.ts
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideRouter, withComponentInputBinding } from '@angular/router';
import { provideHttpClient, withInterceptors } from '@angular/common/http';
import { AppComponent } from './app/app.component';
import { routes } from './app/app.routes';
import { authInterceptor } from './app/auth.interceptor';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding()),
    provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor]))
  ]
});

// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { RouterOutlet } from '@angular/router';
@Component({ selector: 'app-root', standalone: true, imports: [RouterOutlet], template: '<router-outlet />' })
export class AppComponent {}
```

**TL;DR:** @defer lazily loads a template subtree on trigger (idle, viewport, interaction, hover, timer, when) with placeholder/loading/error states.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">@defer: lazy-load template on trigger</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">Triggers</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">idle / viewport / interaction</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">hover / timer(n)</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">when(cond)</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">States</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@placeholder (before)</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@loading (fetching)</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@error</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Benefit</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">bundle split per block</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">prefetch optional</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">less JS upfront</text></svg></div>

`@defer` (Angular 17+) code-splits the template block into a separate chunk loaded only when its trigger fires:

```html
@defer (on viewport; prefetch on idle) {
  <heavy-chart />
} @placeholder { <skeleton /> }
@loading (minimum 300ms) { <spinner /> }
@error { Failed }
```

Triggers: `on idle` (after main work), `on viewport` (IntersectionObserver), `on interaction` (click/keydown), `on hover`, `on timer(2s)`, `when isReady` (custom boolean). `prefetch on idle` downloads ahead of the trigger. Combine with `on immediate` to never defer.

The compiler generates a dynamic import for dependencies inside the block, giving per-block splitting without route boundaries.

| Trigger | Fires when |
|---|---|
| `on idle` | browser idle callback |
| `on viewport` | enters viewport |
| `on interaction` / `hover` | user interacts/hovers |
| `on timer(500)` | after delay |
| `when cond` | boolean becomes true |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I wrap below-fold heavy components in @defer (on viewport; prefetch on idle) — the placeholder shows instantly and the chunk loads only near view.'

```ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
@Component({
  selector: 'app-dashboard',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    @defer (on viewport; prefetch on idle) {
      <heavy-chart [data]="chartData" />
    } @placeholder {
      <div class="skeleton">Chart placeholder</div>
    } @loading (minimum 500ms) {
      <spinner />
    } @error {
      <p>Failed to load chart</p>
    }
  `
})
export class DashboardComponent { chartData = [1,2,3]; }
```

**TL;DR:** New control flow (@if, @for track, @empty, @switch) is compiler-native blocks — faster, no CommonModule, and require tracking.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">New control flow: built-in blocks, no directive import</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">@if / @else</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@if(cond){...}</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@else if / @else</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no *ngIf</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">@for track</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@for(item of list; track item.id)</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@empty fallback</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">keyed diff</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">@switch</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@switch(val){</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">@case / @default</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">exhaustive</text></svg></div>

Angular 17+ introduces built-in control flow compiled to instructions rather than structural directives:

```html
@if (user(); as u) { {{u.name}} } @else { Login }
@for (item of items(); track item.id) { <li>{{item.name}}</li> } @empty { No items }
@switch (status()) { @case ('ok') { OK } @default { ... } }
```

Benefits: ~30% faster creation (no directive lookup), no `CommonModule` import, mandatory `track` in `@for` prevents accidental O(n) identity bugs, and `@empty` handles empty lists declaratively. `@if` supports `as` alias for narrowing. Old `*ngIf`/`*ngFor` remain but are deprecated for new code.

Tooling: `ng generate @angular/core:control-flow` migrates automatically.

| Old | New | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `*ngIf="cond"` | `@if (cond) {...}` | supports `@else` |
| `*ngFor="let x of xs"` | `@for (x of xs; track x.id)` | track required |
| `*ngSwitch` | `@switch/@case/@default` | compiler blocks |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I use @for track item.id — the required track forces stable identity and @empty replaces the extra *ngIf for empty lists.'

```ts
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
@Component({
  selector: 'app-list',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    @if (user(); as u) {
      <h2>{{ u.name }}</h2>
    } @else {
      <button (click)="login()">Login</button>
    }
    <ul>
      @for (item of items(); track item.id) {
        <li>{{ item.name }}</li>
      } @empty {
        <li>No items</li>
      }
    </ul>
  `
})
export class ListComponent {
  user = signal<{name:string}|null>(null);
  items = signal([{id:1,name:'A'}]);
  login(){ this.user.set({name:'Ada'}); }
}
```

**TL;DR:** Angular SSR renders HTML on server; hydration reuses DOM without teardown — ngSkipHydration skips problematic subtrees.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">SSR → hydration: reuse server DOM, attach listeners</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">SSR render</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">renderApplication()</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">emit HTML</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">TransferState inline</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Hydration</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">match DOM nodes</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">attach listeners</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">reuse, not recreate</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#ef4444"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#ef4444">Mismatch</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ngSkipHydration</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">browser-only in afterRender</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">server vs client diff</text></svg></div>

With `ng add @angular/ssr`, `server.ts` calls `renderApplication` producing HTML. The client bundle then hydrates: it walks the existing DOM, matches `ng-server` serialized state, and attaches event listeners without recreating nodes (non-destructive hydration since v17). This yields faster LCP and no flicker.

Mismatches (e.g. `Math.random()` or `window` usage during render) trigger `NG0500` warnings. Fix by guarding browser code with `afterRender`/`afterNextRender` or `isPlatformBrowser`, or add `ngSkipHydration` on a component host to skip that subtree: `<my-chart ngSkipHydration />`.

Use `provideClientHydration()` and `withHttpTransferCacheOptions` to avoid refetching.

| Stage | What happens |
|---|---|
| Server | `renderApplication` → HTML + `TransferState` JSON inline |
| Client hydrate | match nodes, hydrate component tree, attach listeners |
| Mismatch fix | `ngSkipHydration` or move browser code to `afterRender` |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I add provideClientHydration() and guard window usage with afterNextRender; for third-party canvases I add ngSkipHydration to avoid mismatch.'

```ts
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideClientHydration } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideHttpClient, withFetch } from '@angular/common/http';
import { AppComponent } from './app/app.component';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [ provideClientHydration(), provideHttpClient(withFetch()) ]
});

// component with browser-only code
import { afterNextRender } from '@angular/core';
@Component({ selector: 'my-chart', template: '<canvas #c></canvas>', host: { ngSkipHydration: '' } })
export class ChartComponent {
  constructor(){ afterNextRender(()=> initChart()); }
}
```

**TL;DR:** inject() works in any injection context (factory, guard, field initializer); constructor injection is classic — avoid mixing in same class.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">inject() = functional, usable outside constructor</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Constructor</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">constructor(private svc: Svc){}</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">class-only</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">test via TestBed</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">inject()</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">inject(Svc) in field/guard</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">functional guards/interceptors</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">needs injectionContext</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Rule</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">pick one style per file</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">inject in factory/fn</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">constructor for services</text></svg></div>

`constructor(private http:HttpClient){}` is classic DI. `inject(HttpClient)` is a function callable during construction (field initializers, `factory` providers, functional guards/interceptors, `runInInjectionContext`). Before constructors run, the injector is active, so field `foo = inject(Svc)` works.

Advantages of `inject()`: enables functional APIs (`canActivate:[() => inject(Auth).isLogged()]`) and composition without base classes; better with `inject()` in `providers` factory. Must be called synchronously inside injection context — not inside `setTimeout` or after `await` unless wrapped in `runInInjectionContext`.

Style: pick one per class for consistency; many teams prefer `inject()` in standalone components for brevity.

| Aspect | Constructor | `inject()` |
|---|---|---|
| Where | constructor param | field/factory/guard body |
| Context | always injectable | must be injection context |
| Functional guard | awkward (class) | natural `inject(Auth)` |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I use inject() for functional guards/interceptors and field injection, constructor injection for plain services — never after an await without runInInjectionContext.'

```ts
import { inject } from '@angular/core';
import { CanActivateFn } from '@angular/router';
import { AuthService } from './auth.service';

export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () => {
  const auth = inject(AuthService);
  return auth.isLoggedIn();
};

// component field injection
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
@Component({ selector:'app-x', standalone:true, template:'' })
export class XComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient); // field initializer, no constructor
}
```

**TL;DR:** Defer isolates dependencies per block into its own chunk; prefetch hints load ahead without rendering.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Defer chunk isolation + prefetch decode</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Isolation</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">deps inside block only</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">own chunk</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">not in main</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Prefetch</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">prefetch on idle</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">prefetch on interaction</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">prefetch when cond</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Tuning</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">minimum loading time</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">placeholder vs loading</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">avoid layout shift</text></svg></div>

Code inside `@defer` is treeshaken into a separate chunk automatically — imports only used there do not inflate `main.js`. Even if the block never triggers, the code never downloads unless prefetched.

Prefetch is distinct from trigger: `@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle)` downloads on idle but only renders on interaction, giving instant open. Prefetch triggers mirror main triggers (`prefetch on viewport`).

Use `minimum` on `@loading` to avoid spinner flash, and reserve `@placeholder` height to prevent CLS when the deferred component mounts. Analyze chunks via `ng build --stats-json`.

| Pattern | Download | Render |
|---|---|---|
| `@defer (on viewport)` | when in viewport | same time |
| `@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle)` | on idle (early) | on click (instant) |
| No defer | in main bundle | immediately |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I separate heavy deps inside @defer and use prefetch on idle so analytics panel downloads early but only instantiates on interaction.'

```ts
@Component({
  standalone: true,
  imports: [], // HeavyLib NOT imported here
  template: `
    @defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle) {
      <analytics-panel />
    } @placeholder {
      <div style="height:400px">Analytics — click to load</div>
    } @loading (minimum 400ms) {
      <spinner />
    }
  `
})
export class DashboardComponent {}
```

**TL;DR:** track tells Angular how to identity items for minimal DOM moves; @empty replaces manual empty checks.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">@for track = identity function; @empty = declarative empty</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">track</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">track item.id / $index</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">stable identity</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no re-render all</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#ef4444"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#ef4444">No track</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">error at compile</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">forces conscious key</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">perf + bug avoid</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">@empty</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">shown when list empty</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no *ngIf wrapper</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">cleaner template</text></svg></div>

`*ngFor` defaulted to object identity, causing subtle bugs when array was recreated (all DOM recreated). `@for` forces explicit `track item.id` (or `track $index` if truly index-stable), analogous to Vue's `:key` or React's `key`. The compiler uses the track expression to map items to DOM nodes efficiently.

`@empty` is rendered only when the iterable is empty, removing the common `@if (items.length)` wrapper. It composes with async pipes: `@for (u of users$ | async; track u.id) ... @empty { No users }`.

Mistake: using `track $index` on reorderable lists causes state mismatch — always prefer stable id.

| Writing | Result |
|---|---|
| `@for (x of xs; track x.id)` | minimal moves, state preserved |
| `@for (x of xs; track $index)` | correct only if order never changes |
| `*ngFor` without `trackBy` | full re-render on array replace |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I always track by stable id — track $index only for static, non-reorderable lists — and use @empty instead of duplicating the array-check.'

```ts
@Component({
  template: `
    @for (user of users(); track user.id) {
      <user-card [user]="user" />
    } @empty {
      <p>No users found. <button (click)="load()">Reload</button></p>
    }
  `
})
export class UsersComponent {
  users = signal<User[]>([]);
  load(){ /* fetch */ }
}
```

**TL;DR:** input() creates a signal input — required via input.required() and transforms via {transform}; read with this.name().

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Signal inputs: input() / input.required() / computed derivation</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">@Input()</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">decorator, mutable</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ngOnChanges hook</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">non-signal</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">input()</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">signal InputSignal</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">required / alias</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">transform fn</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Usage</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">this.name()</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">computed(()=>name()+'!')</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no ngOnChanges</text></svg></div>

`@Input() name:string` is mutable and needs `ngOnChanges` to react. Signal inputs: `name = input<string>()` / `name = input.required<string>()` / `count = input(0, {alias:'cnt', transform:numberAttribute})`. They return `InputSignal<T>` read as `this.name()`.

Benefits: automatic tracking in `computed`/`effect`, no lifecycle hook, default values as signal initial value, and input transforms (e.g. `booleanAttribute` for `<my-comp disabled>`). `input.required` enforces compile-time presence.

Migration is incremental: mix `@Input` and `input()` in same component, but prefer consistency. Read-only — parent updates flow in; writing inside child is an anti-pattern.

| Feature | `@Input()` | `input()` |
|---|---|---|
| Declaration | `decorator` | `name = input.required<T>()` |
| Read | `this.name` | `this.name()` (signal) |
| Transform | `setter` | `{transform: numberAttribute}` |
| Reactive | `ngOnChanges` | `computed/effect` auto |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'For new components I use input.required with transforms — derived state becomes a computed on the input signal, no ngOnChanges.'

```ts
import { Component, input, computed, booleanAttribute, numberAttribute } from '@angular/core';
@Component({ selector:'app-user', standalone:true, template:'{{ full() }}' })
export class UserComponent {
  firstName = input.required<string>();
  age = input(0, { transform: numberAttribute });
  disabled = input(false, { transform: booleanAttribute });
  full = computed(() => `${this.firstName()} (${this.age()}y)`);
}
```

**TL;DR:** output() returns OutputEmitterRef emitting via .emit(); outputFromObservable bridges RxJS to outputs with auto-unsubscribe.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">output() replaces EventEmitter with typed emitter</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">@Output()</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">EventEmitter<T></text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">manual .emit</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">subscribe cleanup</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">output()</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">OutputEmitterRef<T></text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">emit via .emit()</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no decorator</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">outputFromObservable</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">wrap Observable→output</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">auto unsubscribe</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">interop</text></svg></div>

`@Output() clicked = new EventEmitter<string>()` requires decorator and `EventEmitter`. New API: `clicked = output<string>()` (or `output.required`) returns `OutputEmitterRef` — call `this.clicked.emit(value)` same way, but type `OutputRef` is signal-compatible and works with `outputFromObservable(interval$.pipe(map))` which auto-manages subscription lifetime.

Advantages: no `EventEmitter` import, uniform with signal inputs, and `outputFromObservable` cleanly exposes RxJS streams as outputs without manual `ngOnDestroy` teardown. Template usage unchanged: `(clicked)="onClick($event)"`.

Both coexist; `output()` is preferred for new code.

| API | Declare | Emit | Cleanup |
|---|---|---|---|
| `@Output() e = new EventEmitter()` | decorator | `e.emit(v)` | manual |
| `e = output<T>()` | function | `e.emit(v)` | same, typed emitter |
| `e = outputFromObservable(obs$)` | bridge | obs emits → output | auto on destroy |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I use output() for new components; when I need to expose a stream from a service I use outputFromObservable(stream$) to avoid manual unsubscribe.'

```ts
import { Component, output, outputFromObservable } from '@angular/core';
import { interval, map } from 'rxjs';

@Component({ selector:'app-timer', standalone:true, template:'' })
export class TimerComponent {
  tick = output<number>();
  // bridge observable to output
  mapped = outputFromObservable(interval(1000).pipe(map(v=>v*2)));
  go(){ this.tick.emit(42); }
}
```

**TL;DR:** toSignal subscribes to Observable as signal; toObservable exposes signal as Observable — both manage lifecycle.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">toSignal / toObservable: seamless Signals↔RxJS bridge</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">toSignal(obs$)</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">sync signal value</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">requireSync / initialValue</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">auto unsubscribe</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">toObservable(sig)</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">emit on signal change</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">cold observable</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">pipe operators</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Use</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">HttpClient → Signal</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">Signal → debounceTime</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">effect vs pipe</text></svg></div>

`toSignal(observable$, {initialValue:null, requireSync:false})` subscribes and returns a `Signal` whose value tracks the latest emission, unsubscribing on destroy. `toObservable(mySignal)` creates an Observable emitting on each signal change, so you can `pipe(debounceTime, switchMap)`.

Options: `toSignal` needs `initialValue` or `requireSync:true` if source emits synchronously; `manualCleanup:true` disables auto-unsubscribe. Common use: `users = toSignal(http.get(...), {initialValue:[]})` for signal consumption, and `toObservable(search)` → `pipe(debounceTime, switchMap(fetch))` → `toSignal` loop.

Call inside injection context (constructor/field) unless `injector` passed explicitly.

| Direction | API | Lifecycle |
|---|---|---|
| Observable → Signal | `toSignal(obs$, {initialValue})` | auto unsubscribe |
| Signal → Observable | `toObservable(sig)` | emits on change |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I wrap HttpClient observables with toSignal for template signals, and convert search signal to observable when I need debounce/switchMap operators.'

```ts
import { Component, signal, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { toSignal, toObservable } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { debounceTime, switchMap } from 'rxjs';

@Component({ standalone:true, template:'{{ data() | json }}' })
export class SearchComponent {
  query = signal('');
  http = inject(HttpClient);
  // signal → observable for operators → signal again
  results = toSignal(
    toObservable(this.query).pipe(debounceTime(300), switchMap(q=> this.http.get(`/api?q=${q}`))),
    { initialValue: [] }
  );
}
```

**TL;DR:** afterRender runs every render, afterNextRender runs once next render — both SSR-safe; ngAfterViewInit still runs during SSR (needs guard).

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">afterRender hooks only run in browser</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">ngAfterViewInit</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">runs on server too</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">guard needed</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">legacy view hook</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">afterRender</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">every browser render</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">read/write DOM</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">phase aware</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">afterNextRender</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">once, next render only</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">init 3rd-party lib</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">SSR skipped</text></svg></div>

`ngAfterViewInit` runs during SSR, so `window` access breaks. `afterRender(() => { ... }, {phase:AfterRenderPhase.Read})` and `afterNextRender(()=>initChart())` are Angular 17+ hooks that only execute in the browser, after hydration/render, safe for DOM measurement or initializing non-Angular libs.

`afterRender` runs after every subsequent render (use `phase: Read/Write` to avoid layout thrashing). `afterNextRender` runs once after the next render — ideal for one-time plugin init. Both accept `injector` if called outside injection context.

Rule: use `afterNextRender` for init, `afterRender` for recurring DOM sync, keep `ngAfterViewInit` for view-query logic that is SSR-agnostic.

| Hook | Runs during SSR? | Frequency |
|---|---|---|
| `ngAfterViewInit` | yes | once per view init |
| `afterRender` | no (browser only) | every render |
| `afterNextRender` | no | once, next render |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'For DOM measurement or Charts I use afterNextRender so SSR never touches window — afterRender if I need to sync after each update.'

```ts
import { Component, afterNextRender, afterRender, ElementRef, viewChild } from '@angular/core';
@Component({ selector:'app-chart', standalone:true, template:'<canvas #c></canvas>' })
export class ChartComponent {
  canvas = viewChild<ElementRef>('c');
  constructor(){
    afterNextRender(()=> {
      const el = this.canvas()?.nativeElement;
      if(el) initThirdPartyChart(el);
    });
    afterRender(()=> syncScrollPosition());
  }
}
```

**TL;DR:** Zoneless removes Zone.js monkey-patching; change detection runs only when signals/asyncMarkViewDirty fire — smaller bundle, faster.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Zoneless: no Zone.js, signals drive CD</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">With Zone.js</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">patches async APIs</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">CD after any async</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">~30kb zone</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Zoneless</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">provideExperimental...()</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">signals / markViewDirty</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">explicit triggers</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">Migration</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">remove zone.js import</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">use signals</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">scheduled tasks</text></svg></div>

Zoneless (`providers:[provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection()]` in Angular 18+) removes `zone.js` import. Instead of running change detection after every `setTimeout`/XHR/event, Angular only checks when a signal changes, `markViewDirty()` is called, or `async` pipe resolves. This reduces CD frequency drastically.

Bundle drops ~30kb (no zone), stack traces are cleaner, and coalescing is explicit. Trade-off: libraries that rely on Zone's async patching (some third-party) need manual `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()` or `provideZoneChangeDetection` fallback during migration.

All new projects should evaluate zoneless; Google internally runs hybrid `provideZoneless` experiments.

| Aspect | Zone.js | Zoneless |
|---|---|---|
| Trigger | any async → CD | signal/markDirty only |
| Bundle | + zone.js | smaller |
| Debugging | zoned stacks | cleaner |
| Compat | libs auto | may need markForCheck |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I enable zoneless for green-field apps — initial LCP improves because setTimeout batching no longer triggers needless CD.'

```ts
// main.ts — zoneless bootstrap
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';
import { AppComponent } from './app/app.component';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection()]
});
// remove  import 'zone.js' from polyfills
```

**TL;DR:** Functional interceptors are (req, next)=>Observable — registered ordered via withInterceptors([...]) in provideHttpClient.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Functional interceptor chain: request → chain → backend</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">HttpInterceptorFn</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">(req, next) => next(modified)</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">inject() inside fn</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">plain function</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Registration</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([a,b]))</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">order matters</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">tree-shakable</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Class (legacy)</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">implements HttpInterceptor</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">multi:HTTP_INTERCEPTORS</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">verbose</text></svg></div>

Angular 15+ deprecates `HTTP_INTERCEPTORS` class token for `HttpInterceptorFn`. Example: `export const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req,next)=>{ const token=inject(Auth).token(); return next(req.clone({setHeaders:{Authorization:'Bearer '+token}})); }`.

Register once: `provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([loggingInterceptor, authInterceptor, cacheInterceptor]), withFetch())`. Order is the array order; `next` forwards to the next interceptor or backend. Use `withInterceptorsFromDi()` only during migration.

Advantage: interceptors are tree-shakable functions, easier to test via `TestBed` + `provideHttpClient`, and `inject()` replaces constructor DI.

| Style | Define | Register |
|---|---|---|
| Functional | `export const fn:HttpInterceptorFn = (req,next)=>...` | `withInterceptors([fn])` |
| Class | `class I implements HttpInterceptor { intercept... }` | `HTTP_INTERCEPTORS multi` (deprecated) |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I write functional interceptors and order them logging→auth→retry in provideHttpClient — each is a pure function testable without TestBed module.'

```ts
import { HttpInterceptorFn } from '@angular/common/http';
import { inject } from '@angular/core';
import { AuthService } from './auth.service';
import { catchError, throwError } from 'rxjs';

export const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) => {
  const token = inject(AuthService).token();
  const authReq = token ? req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }}) : req;
  return next(authReq);
};
export const retryInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =>
  next(req).pipe(catchError(e => e.status===429 ? next(req) : throwError(()=>e)));

// main.ts
import { provideHttpClient, withInterceptors, withFetch } from '@angular/common/http';
provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor, retryInterceptor]), withFetch());
```

**TL;DR:** viewChild()/viewChildren()/contentChild() are signal queries — read as signal, auto-updates, typed, no lifecycle timing hack.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Signal queries: viewChild() as Signal</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">@ViewChild</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">decorator, static flag</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">undefined until AfterViewInit</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">manual detectChanges</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">viewChild()</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">function returns Signal</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">required/read variants</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">reactive effect</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Usage</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">el = viewChild('ref')</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">el() maybe undefined</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">computed(() => el()?.focus())</text></svg></div>

Angular 17.3+ adds `viewChild('name')`, `viewChild.required('name')`, `viewChildren`, `contentChild` returning `Signal<T|undefined>`. Unlike `@ViewChild` which is set after `ngAfterViewInit`, signal queries are reactive — you can `effect(()=> console.log(child()))` and it re-runs when the view creates/destroys the child (e.g. inside `@if`).

Query by token: `viewChild(MyComp)`, by template ref: `viewChild<ElementRef>('myEl')`. `viewChildren` returns `Signal<readonly T[]>`. No `static:true/false` flag — timing is handled by signal reactivity. Migration: run `ng g @angular/core:signal-queries`.

Old decorators remain but are discouraged for new code.

| API | Type | When available |
|---|---|---|
| `@ViewChild('x') x` | field `T` | after `ngAfterViewInit` |
| `x = viewChild<ElementRef>('x')` | `Signal<ElementRef\|undefined>` | reactive, effect tracks |
| `x = viewChild.required('x')` | `Signal<T>` (never undefined) | asserts exists |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I use viewChild signal queries so I can derive computed state from the child existence without lifecycle guards.'

```ts
import { Component, viewChild, viewChildren, ElementRef, afterNextRender } from '@angular/core';
@Component({
  selector:'app-parent',
  standalone:true,
  template: `
    <input #inputEl />
    <child-comp />
    @if (show()) { <child-comp #extra /> }
  `
})
export class ParentComponent {
  input = viewChild<ElementRef>('inputEl');
  children = viewChildren(ChildComponent);
  show = signal(false);
  count = computed(()=> this.children().length);
  constructor(){ afterNextRender(()=> this.input()?.nativeElement.focus()); }
}
```

**TL;DR:** CVA still bridges custom controls to Forms API; signal forms (experimental) promise typed, signal-driven validation.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Custom form control: CVA ↔ NgControl</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">ControlValueAccessor</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">writeValue, registerOnChange</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">bridge UI↔FormControl</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">Forms APIs</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">Reactive Forms fine</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">signal forms proposal</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">typed FormGroup</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Signal forms (exp)</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">signal-based validation</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no AbstractControl</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">RFC stage</text></svg></div>

Today you still implement `ControlValueAccessor` for custom inputs to work with `formControlName`: implement `writeValue`, `registerOnChange`/`onTouched`, `setDisabledState`, and provide `NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR`. Angular 18 typed forms (`FormControl<string>`) improve DX.

Signal forms (RFC, experimental behind flag in 18.2) propose `field = signal('')` with validators as computed signals, avoiding `AbstractControl` tree and enabling fine-grained reactivity. Until stable, stick to Reactive Forms but write components using `viewChild` signal queries and `input()/output()` for cleaner APIs.

Tip: use `provideAsControlValueAccessor` helper if library provides it.

| Need | Current | Future |
|---|---|---|
| Custom input | CVA + Reactive Forms | same, signal forms will wrap |
| Typed values | `FormControl<string>` (v14+) | signal field `signal<string>` |
| Validation | `Validators` sync/async | computed signals |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I build custom controls with CVA for now; I keep logic signal-ready so migration to signal forms is just swapping the container.'

```ts
import { Component, forwardRef, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR, ControlValueAccessor } from '@angular/forms';
@Component({
  selector:'app-star-input',
  standalone:true,
  template:`<span (click)="update()">Value: {{ value() }}</span>`,
  providers:[{provide: NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR, useExisting: forwardRef(()=>StarInputComponent), multi:true}]
})
export class StarInputComponent implements ControlValueAccessor {
  value = signal(0);
  onChange: any = ()=>{};
  writeValue(v:number){ this.value.set(v); }
  registerOnChange(fn:any){ this.onChange=fn; }
  registerOnTouched(fn:any){}
  update(){ this.value.update(v=>v+1); this.onChange(this.value()); }
}
```

**TL;DR:** Functional guards are CanActivateFn ()=> boolean/UrlTree using inject(); lazy loads via loadComponent/loadChildren dynamic import.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Functional guards + loadComponent = lean routing</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">CanActivateFn</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">()=>inject(Auth).check()</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no class</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">composable</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">loadComponent</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">loadComponent:()=>import('./c')</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">chunk per route</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no NgModule</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#8b5cf6">Resolve/Deactivate</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ResolveFn</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">CanDeactivateFn</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">same inject pattern</text></svg></div>

Define guard as function: `export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) => inject(AuthService).isLogged() || inject(Router).createUrlTree(['/login'])` — no class, testable by `TestBed.runInInjectionContext`. Register in route: `{ path:'admin', loadComponent:()=>import('./admin.component').then(m=>m.AdminComponent), canActivate:[authGuard] }`.

Similarly `loadChildren` for grouping, `canDeactivate` for dirty-check modals. Use `withComponentInputBinding()` to bind route params/query to `input()` signals automatically: `userId = input<string>()` receives `:userId` param.

Old class guards (`implements CanActivate`) are deprecated.

| Guard type | Class (old) | Functional |
|---|---|---|
| Activate | `class G implements CanActivate` | `CanActivateFn = () => inject(...).check()` |
| Lazy | `loadChildren: './mod#Mod'` | `loadComponent: ()=>import('./c')` |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I use loadComponent with functional guards — route params flow into input() signals via withComponentInputBinding.'

```ts
import { Routes, CanActivateFn } from '@angular/router';
import { inject } from '@angular/core';
import { AuthService } from './auth.service';
import { Router } from '@angular/router';

export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () => {
  return inject(AuthService).isLoggedIn() ? true : inject(Router).createUrlTree(['/login']);
};

export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'admin', loadComponent: ()=> import('./admin/admin.component').then(m=>m.AdminComponent), canActivate:[authGuard] },
  { path: 'user/:id', loadComponent: ()=> import('./user/user.component').then(m=>m.UserComponent) }
];
// UserComponent: id = input.required<string>() auto-filled via withComponentInputBinding()
```

**TL;DR:** Angular 17+ uses esbuild + Vite application builder — 3x faster builds, native ESM, no webpack by default.

<div class="iq-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">New builder: esbuild + Vite replaces webpack</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Old: webpack</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">custom webpack config</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">slow rebuild</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">commonjs</text><path d="M 266 85 H 328" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">New: esbuild+Vite</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">esbuild bundle</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">Vite dev server</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">HMR fast</text><path d="M 526 85 H 588" stroke="currentColor" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Output</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ESM chunks</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">build stats</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">browser esbuild</text></svg></div>

`angular.json` builder changed from `@angular-devkit/build-angular:browser` to `@angular-devkit/build-angular:application` backed by esbuild + Vite. `ng serve` now uses Vite dev server with HMR (~0.5s rebuild vs 3s). `ng build` emits ESM chunks with `esbuild` optimizations and `browser` field resolution.

Custom webpack config now needs `@angular-builders/custom-esbuild` or `extend` hook; most configs migrate to `vite.config` passthrough. Polyfills field moved to `polyfills: ['zone.js']` (omit for zoneless). SSR builder `@angular/build:application` also uses esbuild.

Check via `npm run build -- --stats-json` and analyze with `esbuild-visualizer`.

| Aspect | Webpack builder | Application (esbuild) |
|---|---|---|
| Builder | `:browser` | `:application` |
| Dev server | webpack-dev-server | Vite |
| Speed | baseline | 2-3x faster cold + HMR |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I migrated to application builder — removed custom webpack, kept only Vite plugins, and zoneless removes zone.js from polyfills.'

```ts
// angular.json
{
  "architect": {
    "build": {
      "builder": "@angular-devkit/build-angular:application",
      "options": {
        "outputPath": "dist/app",
        "browser": "src/main.ts",
        "polyfills": ["zone.js"], // remove for zoneless
        "tsConfig": "tsconfig.json"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

