Signals: `component.count.set(5); fixture.detectChanges(); expect(el.textContent).toContain('5')` — also test `computed` derivations. For `input()` set via `fixture.componentRef.setInput('name', 'Ada')`.

Control flow: no `*ngIf` mocking — just assert DOM after signal changes. `@defer` in tests stays in `Placeholder` state by default; advance with `(await DeferBlockFixture)` unstable `render(DeferBlockState.Complete)` in Angular 18 test harness (import from `@angular/core/testing`). Or mock heavy component.

For `toSignal` HTTP, use `provideHttpClient` + `HttpTestingController` or `httpResource` (Angular 19) mock.

Use `TestBed.configureTestingModule({ imports:[MyStandaloneComponent] })` without modules.

| Feature | Test approach |
|---|---|
| `signal` | `sig.set` + `detectChanges` + DOM assert |
| `input()` | `fixture.componentRef.setInput` |
| `@defer` | `DeferBlockFixture render(Complete)` or mock child |

**Interview Tip:** Say: 'I test defer blocks by forcing DeferBlockState.Complete in TestBed — otherwise the deferred chunk never loads in unit tests.'

```ts
import { TestBed } from '@angular/core/testing';
import { ListComponent } from './list.component';

it('shows empty then items', async ()=>{
  const f = TestBed.createComponent(ListComponent);
  f.detectChanges();
  expect(f.nativeElement.textContent).toContain('No items');
  f.componentInstance.items.set([{id:1,name:'A'}]);
  f.detectChanges();
  expect(f.nativeElement.textContent).toContain('A');
});

it('sets signal input', ()=>{
  const f = TestBed.createComponent(ListComponent);
  f.componentRef.setInput('user', {name:'Ada'});
  f.detectChanges();
  // assert
});
```
